<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hegra</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mada'in Salih, al-Hijr, Al-Hijr</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hegra, also known as Mada'in Salih or al-Hijr, is an archaeological site near al-Ula in north-western Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. It was the second city of the Nabataean Kingdom after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a>, and its more than 100 monumental tombs, cut into sandstone outcrops in the 1st century BCE and 1st century CE, are its best-known remains. It became Saudi Arabia's first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2008.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lihyan declined as the Nabataean Kingdom extended its control over the caravan trade and took <b>Hegra</b> (Mada'in Salih) to the north.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lihyan">Kingdom of Lihyan</a></li><li><blockquote>The Quran tells of the Thamud as a people destroyed for rejecting the prophet Salih, and links them to the rock tombs of <b>Hegra</b>, which were in fact Nabataean.</blockquote><a href="/state/thamud">Thamud</a></li></ul></section>
